c or load-bearing:The whitepaper already concedes \"scalar per-memory, not per-relation-type\" and a `:component-limited` corpus. What it doesn't say is that those are the *same fact*. Testing that:Decisive: **71/71 memories attach to exactly one pattern** — `:patterns [pattern]`, singular, in the promotion pipeline. So the corpus isn't *approaching* a connectivity floor; it is by construction 8 disjoint stars, and more memories only make the stars fatter.This is going into the whitepaper as a central claim, so let me verify it against the store rather than the promotion scripts:90/90 confirmed from the store. But the census surfaced something better — let me test it:The measurement lands cleanly. Writing it up — this is the substantive finding:The scribe declined the sibling-transplant rule, and its reason corrects my framing: *\"one verified transplant; the consecutive-use observation is not a second transplant instance.\"* I'd loosely called it two instances — two consecutive *uses* of a memory aren't two *transplants*. It's right.Witness matches my verification exactly, including the character-identical check. Promoting:Promoted but `:proposed` — the review still has to be applied. Applying it:Loop keeps running (run 24 = a92J05, a Rouche beneficiary, `invoke-1785437916706-378-286a4226`). Meanwhile, on the redirection — I ran the census, and it turned up something better than a tidying job.\n\n**The `:component-limited` reading is a property of the reader, not the corpus.**\n\nMeasured from the store, not the scripts: all **90/90** promoted memories attach to **exactly one** pattern, because the promotion pipeline writes `:patterns [pattern]`, singular. Recall issues a single hardcoded `{:type :memory/assert}` edge read. So the graph the operator sees is a disjoint union of **10 stars** — 30, 21, 14, 10, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 1 — with no path longer than 2 anywhere. And it's *closed under adding memories*: more memories make the stars fatter, never connected. Solving more problems cannot fix this.\n\n**But the connectivity is already in the store, on a role the operator doesn't read.** The same edges carry `:subjects` — 133 distinct values over 365 uses, including concrete handles like `connectedComponents_complement_lemniscate_le` (7 memories), `young-convolution-L1-L2` (8):\n\n| edge relation | components | largest |\n|---|---|---|\n| shared **pattern** — what recall traverses | **10** | 30 |\n| shared **subject** | **2** | 87 |\n| concrete handles only | 29 | 11 |\n\nAdmitting one further relation collapses ten components to two, **with no new memories written**.\n\nThat third row is the design answer to your hierarchy question. The concrete-handle relation gives 29 clusters of sizes 11, 8, 7, 6, 5, 5, 4, 4 — technique neighbourhoods that cut across the pattern tier. **The middle tier already exists in the data; it just isn't read.** So the hierarchy is derivable, not an annotation project — which matters, because the fields we *did* annotate rotted: `:level` has 40 distinct values over 91 drafts, 28 singletons, and nothing in the recall path reads it. A field with no consumer has no pressure toward a controlled vocabulary.\n\n**Whitepaper increment written** (`docs/retrieval-whitepaper.md`): a new §5 evidence subsection with the census, a new **§4.1** giving the deployed/measured/proposed hierarchy, and a sharpening of §6 — because two limits it listed separately (\"Ψ is scalar per-relation-type\" and \"corpus is `:component-limited`\") are the same fact seen twice. A single admitted relation *is* what makes it a star forest. I also recorded that the exploration-mass floor is currently vacuous: it constrains a one-element set.\n\nI wrote the retraction condition into §4.1 rather than leaving it implied: **admitting the subject relation should clear the `:component-limited` meter with no new memories. If it doesn't, §4.1 and §5 are wrong and should be retracted.** I haven't run it — that needs a change to the recall path, which under the handoff protocol is a Codex job with me reviewing.\n\nTwo smaller things: scribe pass 24 returned 1 draft and **declined** the sibling-transplant rule, correcting a blur of mine — two consecutive *uses* of a memory aren't two *transplants*. Promoted and reviewed. And promotion still leaves attachments `:proposed`; this morning's fix made them *reviewable*, not *reviewed*, so every pass needs the separate review run or it's silently unrecallable. Folding that in is owed.\n\nWant me to bell out the subject-relation experiment? It's the cheap falsifiable test and it's the difference between §4.1 being a measurement and being a proposal.This park wake is for work I completed in the previous turn — the checklist is already discharged. Confirming state rather than redoing it:All five checklist items were discharged in the previous turn — draft parses with axioms matching the receipt, hook is not a name-restatement, the decline is recorded as a good outcome, promotion committed *and* reviewed (`:reviewed`), run 24 dispatched and running. Nothing to redo.\n\nContinuing the hierarchy work while run 24 is in flight. The falsifiable test needs the *real* connectivity meter, not my own component arithmetic — locating it:The meter's own preregistered criterion #2 is \"at least 2 distinct relation/attachment types\" — which is precisely the thing that's structurally impossible here. Checking which criterion actually fails:λ₂ = **1.0** in the largest component — that's the exact spectral signature of a star K₁,ₙ. And its `:edge-types` is `[:pattern-attachment]`, a single type. So of the meter's three preregistered criteria, **#3 passes, #1 failed on size (6 nodes), #2 fails on relation count**.\n\nCriterion #1 has since cleared — today's largest star is 31 nodes. Let me get a fresh reading, since the script freezes its export by date:Making the frozen paths overridable so the 2026-07-27 artifact stays reproducible while I take a fresh reading:Regression passed — the default stamp re-metered the frozen export, verdict and artifact `:existing` unchanged, so the edit is non-destructive. The fresh read hit SIGTERM at 600s: it queries `limit=5000`, and we measured list reads at 12–24 s for limit=200. Relaunching it durably, and checking it didn't leave a partial export:A partial-or-complete export was left behind — and since the meter is write-once, the relaunch will read *that* rather than the store. Checking its integrity before trusting any verdict from it:Export is **complete**, not truncated: 154 rows, `:truncated? false`, clean parse. The store query finished at 19:00:54 and the kill landed later, in the eigendecomposition. So re-metering that frozen export is valid — 154 `memory/assert` rows today against 62 on 07-27, a 2.5× corpus.Run 24 is done. Getting its report:Run 24 is a **blocked-with-partial**, and its recall miss is directly relevant to the hierarchy work. Verifying both:Verified: sorry 1 → 1, single file touched, no relocation, and the three \"deleted\" lines are prose (the obsolete \"Mathlib does not have Rouché's theorem\" boundary comment), not statements. Corpus correctly unchanged at 79/149.\n\nNow the recall miss, which is a live instance of the star-forest problem:**That is the demonstration.** Six memories are subject-tagged `rouche-root-count-transfer` — exactly run 24's topic — and the pattern tier scatters them across **three disjoint stars**:\n\n- `math/missing-dependency-protocol` — 2, incl. `zeroCountInClosedBall-homotopy-invariant`\n- `math/holomorphic-disk-api` — 2, incl. **`straight-line-rouche-homotopy-stays-nonzero-on-boundary`**\n- `math-formalization/tactic-algebra-interference` — 2\n\nThe runner re-derived straight-line boundary nonvanishing by hand and reported \"no dispatch-time memories were supplied.\" A reviewed memory naming that exact lemma existed and was unreachable, because recall traverses patterns and the technique cluster is split three ways.a02J05's precedent returns a blocked row 